Load Parts with an Exit Control
Load Parts with an Exit Control
In this sample model we will solve this modeling task:
• The PickAndPlace robot picks a Part up from a Source and places it directly into a Basket.
• Conveyor1 transports the Basket to the Station which processes the Part.
• Conveyor2 transports the Basket containing the Part in the opposite direction.
• Once the Basket stopped, the PickAndPlace robot picks up the Part and places it in the Drain.
The Variable named WaitingBasket ensures that the part will always be put into the correct basket and
that the PickAndPlace robot only picks up a part when a basket is available to place the part.
• We accomplish this with the Exit Control and the Target Control of the PickAndPlace robot.
In our example we will:
• Model the Material Flow and Program the Controls
• Configure the Robot and Program Exit and Target Control
The finished simulation model looks like this:
Load Parts with an Exit Control
Plant Simulation Help 10-659
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Pick and Place Parts with the PickAndPlace Robot
Model the Material Flow and Program the Controls
As we do not want to modify the built-in MU classes, we duplicate the Part and the Container in the folder
MUs. We renamed the duplicated Part to MyPart and the Container to Basket.
Then we insert the material flow objects.
• The SourceParts produces a part of type MyPart every 30 seconds.
Model the Material Flow and Program the Controls
10-660 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

In the Exit Control of the SourcePart we set the destination of MyPart. We named our control
exitSourcePart and typed in this source code:
// exit control of the SourceParts
var Destination: object := Robot.getDestination
stopuntil WaitingBasket /= void and Destination /= WaitingBasket
@.Destination := WaitingBasket
@.move
• We selected these settings in the Variable named WaitingBasket.
The Variable named WaitingBasket ensures that the part will always be put into the correct basket and
that the PickAndPlace robot only picks up a part when a basket is available to place the part.
• The SourceBasket produces 4 baskets in an interval of 10 seconds.
Model the Material Flow and Program the Controls
Plant Simulation Help 10-661
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• The conveyors transport the Basket with the loaded part to type MyPart through the plant. We inserted
Conveyor1 from right to left and Conveyor2 from left to right. In addition we created a sensor each with
the respective control.
For the sensor on Conveyor1 we selected these settings.
Model the Material Flow and Program the Controls
10-662 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We typed in this source code into the Sensor Control. It sets how MyPart is unloaded from the Basket
and ensures that the Basket waits at the sensor until a new part is loaded, i.e. it is not empty any more.
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean, BookPos: boolean
@.Stopped := true
WaitingBasket := @
stopuntil not @.Empty
WaitingBasket := void
@.Stopped := false
For the sensor on Conveyor2 we selected these settings and selected our unloadCtrl as the control.
In the sensor control of Conveyor2 we set how the part will be unloaded from the Basket. We typed in
this source code:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean, BookPos: boolean
var MU : object
@.Stopped  := true // stop
var Destination:object := Robot
MU := @.Cont
MU.Destination := Drain
if not MU.move(Destination)
   stopuntil MU.Location /= @ and not Destination.IsLoading 
end
@.Stopped := false
Model the Material Flow and Program the Controls
Plant Simulation Help 10-663
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Load Parts with an Exit Control
Configure the Robot and Program Exit and Target Control
In this step we configure the PickAndPlace robot and program the Exit Control and the Target Control.
First, we set the angles at which the PickAndPlace robot picks the parts up, places them, and deposits
them:
• Drag Conveyor1 first and then Conveyor2 onto the picture of the PickAndPlace robot and drop it there.
• Connect the SourceParts with a Connector with the PickAndPlace robot. Then connect the PickAndPlace
robot with the Drain.
• Double-click the picture of the PickAndPlace robot and open the Angles Table. You can adjust the
angles if they are not precise enough.
• Open the Angles Table. We use these time. You can adjust them if need be.
Then, we select the controls which define how the parts are loaded and to which destination they move:
• We selected our loadCtrl as the Exit Control.
In the Exit Control we set how the PickAndPlace robot loads the part onto Conveyor1 if that is its
destination.
Configure the Robot and Program Exit and Target Control
10-664 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We typed in this source code:
// exit control of the Robot
if @.Destination = Conveyor1
   stopuntil WaitingBasket /= void
   @.move(WaitingBasket)
else
   @.move
end
• As Target Control we selected the targetCtrlRobot.
In the Target Control we set the target of the part.
We typed in this source code:
// target control of the Robot
if @.Destination = Conveyor1 and WaitingBasket /= void
   ?.setDestination(WaitingBasket)
else
   ?.setDestination(@.Destination)
end
• We selected Exit strategy or target control as the Target Selection.
Configure the Robot and Program Exit and Target Control
Plant Simulation Help 10-665
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When we run the simulation, we see that the PickAndPlace robot picks MyPart up at the SourceParts,
rotates toward Conveyor1 and places it in the Basket at the sensor.
Conveyor1 transports the Basket with the loaded part to the Station where it is processed for 10 seconds.
It then moves the Basket with the loaded part to Conveyor2 where the PickAndPlace robot unloads it,
rotates toward the Drain, and deposits it there.
After we ran the simulation for the first time, we changed the settings of the Basket and it graphic.
We finally moved the objects closer to each other so that the model looks more realistic.
Configure the Robot and Program Exit and Target Control
10-666 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

