Back to Load and Unload Parts at the Sensor of a Conveyor
Set the Conveying Direction of the Parts
In this model we show how you can set the MU conveying direction in the PickAndPlace robot. The
conveying direction is of importance because the objects Turnplate, Turntable, AngularConverter, and
Conveyor change the conveying direction of the MUs in the simulation model.
As soon as they do this, Plant Simulation uses the previous length of the MU as its new width and the
previous width as the new length. The PickAndPlace robot can also pick the part up and rotate it arbitrarily
and then place it as it is required for further processing/conveying, just as a robot does in a real factory.
In our model the Source produces parts and moves them on to a Conveyor. The AngularConverter conveys
the part around the corner and changes the conveying direction of the part from Forward to Lateral
right. The PickAndPlace robot picks the part up and places it onto the Conveyor in the direction, which we
selected as the MU Conveying Direction for the PickAndPlace robot. The Conveyor moves the part to the
Drain, which removes it from the installation.
The finished simulation model looks like this:
Set the Conveying Direction of the Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-681
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To create the simulation model, insert the objects so that the match the figure above.
• Our Source uses the default settings.
• Our Conveyor is 6 meters long and is non-accumulating. The gap between the individual parts that are
conveyed is 1 meter.
• Our AngularConverter has an Entry Length and an Exit Length of 2 meters each. We did not change
any other settings.
Set the Conveying Direction of the Parts
10-682 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• In the PickAndPlace robot we selected the MU Conveying Direction > Forwards.
• Our second Conveyor, named Conveyor1 is 5 meters long and is Accumulating.
Set the Conveying Direction of the Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-683
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Our Drain uses the default settings.
Start the simulation and watch how the PickAndPlace robot picks the parts from the AngularConverter,
how it rotates to Conveyor1, and places the part there with the MU Conveying Direction > Forwards.
Here the robot rotates the part so that it will be placed forward on the successor and then places it on the
successor.
You can run further experiments with the different settings for the MU Conveying Direction in the model
and thus find the setting that best fits your modeling needs.
Set the Conveying Direction of the Parts
10-684 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Pick and Place Parts with the PickAndPlace Robot
Unload Stacked Parts
Unload Stacked Parts
The Z-Dimension of the Store, the Z-Dimension of the Transporter, and the Z-Dimension of the Container
allow you to stack parts on them.
In our sample model a Transporter transports pallets on which boxes are stacked. The boxes themselves
contain three stacked parts.
The first PickAndPlace robot unloads the pallets from the Transporter and places them onto a conveyor.
The second PickAndPlace robot unloads the boxes from the pallets and places them onto the second
conveyor.
The third PickAndPlace robot unloads the stacked parts from the boxes and places them onto the third
conveyor which moves them on. A Drain at the respective end of the three conveyors removes the parts
from the plant.
The finished simulation model looks like this:
Unload Stacked Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-685
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To let the robots know where to unload and load parts, drag the respective predecessor and successor
onto the respective robot and drop it there. The Angles Tables of the robots then show the respective
predecessor and successor.
In the following you will:
• Configure the Transporter, the Pallet, and the Box
• Configure the Source and the Track
• Configure the Conveyors
• Configure the Robots Unloading the Stacks
Unload Stacked Parts
10-686 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Pick and Place Parts with the PickAndPlace Robot
Configure the Transporter, the Pallet, and the Box
As the Transporter, the Pallets, and the boxes are always going to be the same, we configure their classes
in the Class Library.
• For the Transporter select the settings below:
Use the standard setting of 1 for the Z-Dimension of the storage area of type Store. Thus, the
Transporter does not stack any Pallets one onto the other.
Configure the Transporter, the Pallet, and the Box
Plant Simulation Help 10-687
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• For the Container, our Pallet, select the settings below. We entered 3 as the Z-Dimension; this way the
Pallet can stack 3 Boxes onto each other.
• To create the box, duplicate the Container in the Class Library. Then rename the duplicated object to Box
and select the settings below. We entered 3 as the Z-Dimension, this way three parts can be stacked in
the Box. The Length and Width make sure that four Boxes fit on a Pallet.
Configure the Transporter, the Pallet, and the Box
10-688 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Unload Stacked Parts
Configure the Source and the Track
In this step we configure the Source, which creates the parts.
Proceed like this:
• Create 10 Transporters every 40 seconds.
Configure the Source and the Track
Plant Simulation Help 10-689
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Determine the parts that are loaded onto the Transporter in the Entrance Control.
First, set the Length of the Transporter to 3.2 meters. You could also have done this in the class
of the Transporter in the Class Library. We recommend to not change the standard settings of the
built-in objects if possible. If you customize them and then merge models later on or if you update the
class library later on, your customized settings are going to be discarded and replaced by the standard
settings. You then have to make your customizations again.
Then, create a Pallet and continue creating Boxes on it until the Pallet is filled with Boxes. In the last step
we are going to produce parts until the Boxes are full as well.
Configure the Source and the Track
10-690 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The respective source code looks like this:
@.Length := 3.2
while not @.Full
   var pallet := .MUs.Container.create(@)
   while not pallet.Full
       var box := .MUs.Box.create(pallet)
       while not box.Full
       .MUs.Part.create(box)
       end
   end
end
Then, configure the Track on which the Transporter drives.
• First, create a sensor at which the robot unloads the Pallets from the Transporter.
Configure the Source and the Track
Plant Simulation Help 10-691
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Next, create the Sensor Control which stops the Track until the Transporter has been unloaded all the
way. Then the Transporter drives on and is going to be removed from the installation in the Drain.
The respective source code looks like this:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean
@.stopped := true
var Destination:object := PickAndPlace
while not @.Empty
   var mu: object := @.cont
   if not mu.move(Destination)
       stopuntil mu.location /= @ and not Destination.isLoading
   end
end
@.stopped := false
Back to Unload Stacked Parts
Configure the Conveyors
In this step we are going to configure the Conveyors that transport the Pallets, the Boxes, and the Parts.
• Insert three Conveyors next to each other.
• For the two Conveyors on the left create two sensors each. For the Conveyor on the right create one
sensor. The robots unload parts at the first sensor from a Conveyor and load them onto the next
Configure the Conveyors
10-692 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Conveyor at the second sensor. The sensors for unloading parts from a Conveyor are triggered by a light
barrier by the rear of the part.
• Then, program the Sensor Controls at which the individual Conveyors load the parts onto the next
Conveyor.
Configure the Conveyors
Plant Simulation Help 10-693
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Except for the Destination the Sensor Control is the same for the three Conveyors. You only have to
adjust the variable Destination, namely PickAndPlace1 and PickAndPlace2.
The respective source code looks like this:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean
@.stopped := true
var Destination:object := PickAndPlace1
while not @.Empty
   var mu: object := @.cont
   if not mu.move(Destination)
       stopuntil mu.location /= @ and not Destination.isLoading
   end
end
@.stopped := false
Configure the Conveyors
10-694 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Unload Stacked Parts
Configure the Robots Unloading the Stacks
In this step we configure the robots that unload the stacks. Our three robots load the parts at the sensors
from the Track onto the Conveyor and then onto the other two Conveyors.
Configure the robots with the same settings. Enter a Loading Time and an Unloading Time of 2 seconds
each. The robot places the parts at the respective first sensor onto the succeeding Conveyor.
Configure the Robots Unloading the Stacks
Plant Simulation Help 10-695
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Double the size of the graphic of the first robot by selecting a Uniform Scaling Factor of 2.
Configure the Robots Unloading the Stacks
10-696 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

