Animate the Robot Arm in 3D
Animate the Robot Arm
On the tab Robot Arm Animation you can set how the arm of the PickAndPlace robot will be animated.
We demonstrate this with a simple sample model.
We will:
• Configure the Material Flow
• Exchange the Graphic of the Robot
• Configure the Movement of the Robot Arm
Back to Pick and Place Parts with the PickAndPlace Robot
Configure the Material Flow
In our first step we model the material flow. To so, we place the individual material flow objects next to
each other to make Plant Simulation connect them automatically with Connectors.
Procedure
1. Our material flow consists of a Source which produces the parts.
2. The first Station processes the parts.
3. After the parts are processed all the way, the Robot picks them up.
4. It then rotates to the seconds Station and places them there. Station1 processes the parts as well.
5. The Drain removes the parts from the installation.
Animate the Robot Arm in 3D
10-698 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We use the material flow objects with their default settings meaning we do not change anything.
The finished simulation model looks like this:
Back to Animate the Robot Arm
Exchange the Graphic of the Robot
As we do not want to use the graphic of the built-in PickAndPlace robot, we will replace its graphic.
• To do so, we click the robot with the right mouse button and select Exchange Graphics.
• We use the PickAndPlace robot by Comau.
Exchange the Graphic of the Robot
Plant Simulation Help 10-699
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Our model now looks like this.
Back to Animate the Robot Arm
Video on YouTube
https://youtu.be/K-NEPvShU10?si=gOBbox6eKxZQNEi8&t=730
Configure the Movement of the Robot Arm
When you run the simulation with the standard settings of the EventController, it runs so fast that it is hard
to recognize the motion sequence. To prevent this, enter 1 as the Real-time Factor.
Video on YouTube
10-700 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Now you can watch the robot move from the default orientation to the Station, lower the robot arm, pick
the part up, raise the robot arm, rotate to the second Station, and place the part there.
You will notice though that the robot arm moves through the protective cage and the parts holder. To
prevent this from happening, change the movement of the robot arm so that it moves above both.
To do so, press the spacebar to open the dialog Edit 3D Properties and change to the tab Robot Arm
Animation [PickAndPlace].
Add the animation path From ‘Station’ to ‘Station1’.
Configure the Movement of the Robot Arm
Plant Simulation Help 10-701
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Then, click Show to show the animation path in the model window.
Next, click Extend and add three path anchor points.
Then adjust the elevation of the anchor points: Click Edit and enter precise values. To do so, double-click
the row of the respective anchor point in the dialog Path Anchor Points and type in an elevation of 2.2
for all anchor points. As our stations are 2 meters high, 2.20 meters should provide enough room for the
robot arm to move across them.
Configure the Movement of the Robot Arm
10-702 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

As the last step you have to move the anchor points to the correction positions on the stations at which
the robot picks the parts up, exits the station, enters the next station and places the part. To do so, click
the respective anchor point and drag it to the correct position.
Configure the Movement of the Robot Arm
Plant Simulation Help 10-703
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When you run the simulation you see that the robot lifts the part above the protective cage while moving
from the Station to Station1.
As our robot arm still moves through the parts holder and the protective cage from the default orientation
to the Station, add the path Default Orientation to Station.
To do so, repeat the steps described above. To prevent the robot arm from touching the indicator light in
the left corner of the cage, we entered 3.8 meters as the elevation. It then looks like this:
Configure the Movement of the Robot Arm
